[Term]
id: HP:0031393
name: Abnormal CD8+ T cell proportion
synonym: "شذوذ في نسبة خلايا تي CD8+" EXACT [PAVS:AR]
synonym: "تغير في نسبة خلايا تي CD8+" RELATED [PAVS:AR]
comment: Arabic Definition: زيادة أو نقصان غير طبيعي في خلايا تي CD3+CD8+ السامة، يتم قياسها كنسبة مئوية من إجمالي خلايا تي CD3+ في الدم، بالمقارنة مع نطاق مرجعي للجنس والفئة العمرية المعطاة. يتم عادة قياس هذه الخلايا ضمن السكان الإيجابي لمستقبل TCR ألفا/بيتا.
def: "Abnormal increase or decrease of cytotoxic CD3+CD8+ T cells, measured as percentage of total CD3+ T cells in the blood, compared to a reference range for a given sex and age-group. These are usually measured within the TCR alpha/beta positive population." []
synonym: "Abnormal proportion of CD8+ T cells" EXACT []
synonym: "Abnormal proportion of CD8-positive T cells" EXACT []
synonym: "Abnormal proportion of CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cells" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0025540 ! Abnormal T cell subset distribution
property_value: dc:date "2017-09-02T17:06:06Z" xsd:dateTime
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:0031394
name: Abnormal CD4:CD8 ratio
synonym: "شذوذ في نسبة CD4:CD8" EXACT [PAVS:AR]
synonym: "تغير في نسبة خلايا CD4 إلى CD8" RELATED [PAVS:AR]
comment: Arabic Definition: أي شذوذ في الكمية النسبية لليمفاويات تي CD4+ و CD8+.
def: "Any abnormality in the relative amount of CD4+ and CD8+ T lymphocytes." [PMID:29095912]
comment: CD4 helper/inducer cells and CD8 cytotoxic/suppressor cells are 2 phenotypes of T lymphocytes, characterized by distinct surface markers and functions that mostly reside in lymph nodes but also circulate in the blood. The normal CD4/CD8 ratio in healthy hosts is poorly defined. Ratios between 1.5 and 2.5 are generally considered normal; however, a wide heterogeneity exists because sex, age, ethnicity, genetics, exposures, and infections may all impact the ratio.
is_a: HP:0025540 ! Abnormal T cell subset distribution
property_value: dc:date "2017-09-02T17:06:44Z" xsd:dateTime
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:0031396
name: Abnormal naive T cell proportion
synonym: "خلل في نسبة الخلايا التائية الأولية" EXACT [PAVS:AR]
synonym: "نسبة غير طبيعية من الخلايا التائية الأولية" RELATED [PAVS:AR]
comment: Arabic Definition: أي خلل في نسبة الخلايا التائية الأولية مقارنة بعدد الخلايا التائية الكلي.
def: "Any abnormality in the proportion of naive T cells relative to the total number of T cells." [PMID:12133935]
comment: Following the development of T cells in the thymus, they enter the blood stream and recirculate between the blood and peripheral lymphatic tissues. Mature recirculating T cells that have not yet encountered their specific antigen are known as naive T cells. Mature T cell not yet exposed to antigen with the phenotype CCR7-positive, CD45RA-positive, and CD127-positive. This cell type is also described as being CD25-negative, CD62L-high and CD44-low. Human mature CD4+ T cells are functionally heterogeneous and can be divided into naive and memory/effector populations based on the expression of cell surface markers. Although the most significant differences between these two populations are functional, the leukocyte common Ag (CD45) was promoted as a potential marker of memory T cells. CD45 Ag may be found in various isoforms depending on alternative splicing of three extracellularly expressed exons. The expression of the highest molecular weight isoform, referred to as CD45RA, defines the population of CD4+ unprimed lymphocytes. Activation of naive lymphocytes through TCR engagement is followed by loss of CD45RA Ag and transition to the expression of the smallest molecular weight isoform CD45RO. These primed cells express high levels of activation Ags, acquire effector functions, and need less costimuli requirements for further activation.
synonym: "Abnormal proportion of naive T cells" EXACT []
synonym: "Abnormal proportion of naive thymus-derived, alpha-beta T cells" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0025540 ! Abnormal T cell subset distribution
property_value: dc:date "2017-09-03T12:46:29Z" xsd:dateTime
